## Decisions

**Why an explicit "## Project Structure" section for a one-file rewrite.**
The plan-aide.md format is unconditional: every plan, regardless of size,
includes the complete annotated tree of its scope directory. The benefit
even for a single-file rewrite is that the implementor sees at a glance
that no other files in the scope directory move or get added — the
rewrite is surgically scoped. Skipping the section because "it's just
one file" would be a malformed plan.

**Why Mode 1 gets a dedicated step (Step 8) instead of being implicit.**
The spec's desired #8 says Mode 1 must remain "untouched in spirit." The
verb is preservation, not edit. A dedicated verification step makes it
explicit that an implementor reading top-to-bottom should NOT touch
Mode 1's behavior, and only sweep terminology where it overlaps with
Mode 2's contract. Without a step naming Mode 1, an implementor doing a
file-wide sweep might inadvertently restructure it.

**Why the Bash-sync step gets its own number (Step 4) rather than being
folded into Step 3.** Two reasons. First, Step 4 runs unconditionally on
THREE state branches (`no-brain-aide` after scaffolding, `no-mcp-entry`,
`mcp-drift`), but Step 3 (scaffolding) only runs on one of those
(`no-brain-aide`). Folding sync into Step 3 would force the Step 1
branch table to either restate the sync recipe twice (once for
`no-mcp-entry`, once for `mcp-drift`) or to point those branches at "the
sync part of Step 3," which is awkward. Separating the steps lets the
branch table point cleanly at Step 4 from three different branches.
Second, Step 4 has its own failure-handling gate (Bash sync exited
non-zero) which is structurally distinct from Step 3's failure
(`aide_init` threw); separating them keeps the failure-handling prose
clean.

**Why Step 5's post-sync message is a single user-facing message, not a
two-step manual remediation.** This is the central architectural shift
the spec encodes (desired #6, undesired #7). The agent runs sync; the
user's remaining work is exactly the restart, because the restart is
the one step the agent genuinely cannot do on the user's behalf
(Claude Code reads `.mcp.json` only at session startup). Splitting the
message into "run sync (which I just did), then restart" would leak the
agent's prior step into the user's todo list and add gratuitous friction.

**Why the prompt must NOT re-call `aide_info` after sync.** The brain
MCP server in the running session was launched before the brain entry
existed; no amount of disk mutation hot-reloads it. `aide_info` reports
the boot reporter's view of disk state, which after sync IS `ok` — but
that report does not reflect the live server's wiring, which is still
unwired. A re-check that returns `ok` here is false confidence; an
agent that "confirmed" before continuing would hit a brain server that
is operationally broken for the rest of the session. The spec encodes
this trap explicitly (desired #6, undesired #9); the prompt names it
explicitly so a future maintainer reading the prompt understands why
the STOP is load-bearing rather than ritualistic.

**Why the launcher is recovered from `.mcp.json` rather than hardcoded
in prose.** The prompt must work uniformly for prod installs (where the
launcher is `cmd /c npx @aidemd-mcp/server`), local-dev installs (where
it's `node D:/path/to/dist/index.js`), and any custom binary path a
user might have. Hardcoding either invocation breaks the others.
Recovering the launcher from `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]` is
guaranteed to work in every install shape because the recovered entry
IS the entry Claude Code itself used to start the AIDE MCP server in
the current session — its existence is provable from the fact that the
prompt is running.

**Why path validation prose is removed entirely (no `invalid-path`
replacement).** The schema collapse retired `rootPath` AND its
validation. Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP server
startup, not the package's. The prompt has nothing useful to say about
"the path doesn't resolve" because the package doesn't validate paths
anymore — and re-introducing language about "fix the path in
`brain.aide`" would teach the agent to mutate fields that no longer
exist (the path lives inline in `mcpServerConfig.args`, and
introspecting args is a hard regression per handoff invariant #11).
The four-state model has no fifth state because the package has no
fifth source of failure to surface.

**Why the spec's body outcome about `mcp-drift` matching the
post-scaffold path drives folding both branches into Steps 4 + 5
rather than each having its own remediation block.** The disk state on
`mcp-drift` is correct; the only thing wrong is `.mcp.json`. The
remediation is exactly the same as the post-scaffold case: run sync,
ask for restart. Maintaining two prose blocks for the same recipe
would invite drift between them. Step 1's branch table names both
states distinctly (so the user's understanding of WHY they got there
is preserved), but their REMEDIATION routes through one shared pair
of steps.
